//? Title: PyramidNet: Gradual Width and Parameter-Free Shortcuts
//? Subtitle: Tensor computations, explicit checks and reproducible local figures
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
PyramidNet widens at every residual unit rather than only at stage boundaries. We inspect
the rounded additive schedule, signed pooled/zero-padded shortcuts, ten full architecture budgets
and complete 272-layer gradients, then explicitly fit a shallow CIFAR PyramidNet-20 with tensor Adam.
)
\toc

Source example: `examples/cpp/source/nn/1_convolutional_neural_networks/1.3_residual_and_dense_families/pyramidnet.cpp`.

> The method

For N units, start running width at 16 for CIFAR or 64 for ImageNet, add alpha/N before
each unit, then round half up. Basic units emit this width; bottlenecks emit four times it.
The CIFAR-110 basic alpha-48 schedule has 54 units, beginning 17/18/19 and ending 64.
Unlike doubling at stage boundaries, additive growth distributes width changes through depth.

$$w_i=\lfloor\operatorname{running}_i+0.5\rfloor,\qquad
 y_i=F_i(x_i)+\operatorname{zero\_pad}(\operatorname{avg\_pool}_{s_i}(x_i)).$$
\alttext(Widths grow additively; shortcuts pool spatially and append zero channels without learned weights.)

Basic order is BN/conv/BN/ReLU/conv/BN; bottleneck order is
BN/1×1/BN/ReLU/3×3/BN/ReLU/1×1/BN. The first BN has no ReLU and nothing gates the
sum. Shortcuts have no parameters: stride-two averages nonoverlapping 2×2 windows, then
appends zero channels. Even sides are required at downsampling to align this VALID pooling
with branch geometry. A zero final BN yields the signed shortcut exactly, with weighted
input derivatives matching independent strided sums (a 1/4 spread for pooled pixels).

The full CIFAR-110 alpha-48 basic model has 1772706 learned and 13168 moving scalars.
CIFAR depths are 6n+2 basic or 9n+2 bottleneck; ImageNet uses four bottleneck stages,
with depth 200 using 3/24/36/3 units. BN learns gamma/beta with epsilon 1e-5. CIFAR's
stem is conv/BN without ReLU; ImageNet adds ReLU/pool. Both end in BN/ReLU, global mean
and a linear classifier. He-uniform fan-in replaces the release's normal fan-out recipe.
Paper parameter tables exclude BN scale/shift: subtracting the moving-state count removes
exactly the same number of learned BN scalars. Count-only ImageNet forwards below use 64
pixels because parameter counts are independent of spatial dimensions.

A complete full-width PyramidNet-272 alpha-200 graph uses 90 bottlenecks and ends at 864
channels. The backward probe keeps every unit but uses 16-pixel synthetic inputs to bound
activation storage. Some shifts have analytically zero gradients in training: the stem BN
and each first/last unit BN shift add channel constants removed by a later BN. These 181
redundant directions are checked separately from other parameter derivatives; zero gradients
there do not imply disconnected filters. Moving updates are disabled for the diagnostic.

The visible fit uses the same alpha-48 basic design at depth 20 (three units per stage,
310845 learned and 2368 moving scalars), so the documentation build stays short; the
budget and gradient checks above cover the full-depth models. It uses 128 fitting images,
64 validation images, 64 official-test images, 5 batch-eight epochs of Adam(.001), explicit
kernel L2=.0001/2 and independent padded crops/flips. Validation selects paired learned/moving
state before testing. The original source's larger short run and the paper's 300-epoch SGD
experiment are different; this bounded fit demonstrates optimization, not published accuracy.

The editable concept diagram is `diagrams/pyramidnet_concept.tikz`. Regeneration uses
LaTeX with PGF/TikZ and standalone, plus dvisvgm; exports use the saved SVG.

```{bash, results="output", file="figures/pyramidnet_concept.svg"}
set -eu
mkdir -p build
latex -interaction=batchmode -halt-on-error -output-directory=build diagrams/pyramidnet_concept.tikz > build/pyramidnet_concept.compile.log 2>&1
dvisvgm --no-fonts --bbox=10 --output=build/pyramidnet_concept.svg build/pyramidnet_concept.dvi > build/pyramidnet_concept.render.log 2>&1
printf 'Rendered local concept diagram.\n'
```
